In some of my recent tinkering with vanilla 3.4.3, I've turned up a pair
of very badly worded possible death messages, which I consider to be
bugs.  Following are the highlights of my report to the DevTeam:

> [...] Essentially, the chain of code for being lifesaved while drowning
> or burning while there are almost no safe tiles on the level, after
> producing the message "You're still drowning/burning", [...] results
> in the unfortunate death messages "Grunt, drowned in a drowning" and
> "Grunt, burned by burning".
> 
> This could be reproduced thus:
> 
> 1. Locate a level with either pools of water or pools of molten lava;
> the more the better.
> 2. Dig out the level and dig to fill as much of the level with
> water/lava as possible.
> 3. On any remaining tiles, create a large amount of monsters (so as to
> minimise the number of possible destination tiles).
>   (These three steps could be simplified by setting up a special level
>    to reproduce these conditions; I simply filled in most of the Plane
>    of Fire - and later replaced the lava with water - to test these
>    cases).
> 4. Wear an amulet of life saving, and set yourself up such that you will
> burn/drown if you step into the lava/water (not fire resistant, stressed
> via cursed loadstones, etc.)
> 5. Step into the water or lava.
>    - "You fall into the water!  You sink like a rock.
>       You try to crawl out of the water. But in vain.
>       You drown. But wait![...]
>       You're still drowning."
>       "Grunt, drowned in a drowning."
>    - "You fall into the lava! You burn to a crisp... But wait![...]
>       You're still burning."
>       "Grunt, burned by burning."

If anyone is willing to try this under real game conditions, please do
so, but it's probably most easily reproducible in wizmode (and even then
only with great amounts of paitence).


